// The cloud shadow map: what one texel of it MEANS, how it is produced, and how a point anywhere in the
// world reads a correct transmittance back out of it with a single fetch.
//
// THE ONE IDEA THIS FILE EXISTS FOR. A texel does not store "shadowed / not shadowed", and it does not
// store a depth to compare against. It stores a TRIPLE:
//
//     R  FrontDepthKm          how far along the light ray the first material was found
//     G  MeanExtinctionPerKm   the mean extinction of the samples that found material
//     B  MaxOpticalDepth       the optical depth of the WHOLE ray through the shell
//
// From those three, the transmittance at an ARBITRARY depth d along that same ray is
//
//     tau(d) = min( MaxOpticalDepth, MeanExtinction * max(0, d - FrontDepth) )      T = exp(-tau)
//
// which is correct for a point BELOW the layer, for a point INSIDE it, and for a point ABOVE it — one
// fetch, no second pass, no cascade. That is what an ordinary depth-compare shadow map cannot do at all:
// a medium has no surface to compare a depth against, and "is the receiver behind the front of the
// cloud" answers the wrong question for everything that is in the cloud or above its base. The far term
// is the clamp: past the far side of the layer the ray has met all the material there is, so the optical
// depth stops growing instead of extrapolating a mean extinction through clear air.
//
// This is Unreal's encoding, and it is transplanted rather than re-derived. The producer is
// VolumetricCloud.usf:2168-2215 (`MainPS` under SHADER_SHADOW_PS) and the consumer is
// VolumetricCloudCommon.ush:54-68 (`GetCloudVolumetricShadow`). Licence position:
// Docs/Clouds/LICENCE_RECORD.md, ticket #UE-2026-0842-WAIVER.
//
// UNITS: KILOMETRES and per-kilometre, throughout, which is this subsystem's convention
// (Common/CloudGeometry.glslh). Unreal stores metres and 1/m; converting would buy nothing and would put
// one more factor of a thousand into a subsystem that has already paid for one.
//
// DIALECT: the rules of Common/CloudNoise.glslh — no swizzles past .x/.y/.z, no `out` parameters, no
// arrays, no `inline`, no namespaces, `f`-suffixed literals, structs returned through a named local, every
// function defined before it is used. This file is compiled AS C++ by Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudShadow, and
// it is included by BOTH the producer (Programs/Clouds/CloudShadowMap.shader) and the consumer
// (Programs/Deferred/DeferredLighting.shader) — which is the point: the encode and the decode are two
// halves of one relation and they are written once.

#ifndef CLOUD_SHADOW_MAP_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_SHADOW_MAP_GLSLH

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FOUR NUMBERS THAT SIZE THE MAP, and the chain that fixes them
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// They are engine constants rather than component fields for the reason the step schedule is
// (Common/CloudGeometry.glslh): they trade cost against quality identically in every scene, which is why
// Unreal carries them as cvars and not as properties of UVolumetricCloudComponent. What IS authorable is
// whether the layer casts at all and how strong the shade is — VolumetricCloudData::CastShadows and
// ::ShadowStrength.
//
// THEY ARE THE REFERENCE TIER'S VALUES, and a quality tier scales the first three of them TOGETHER by one
// factor (Core::CloudQuality, Graphic::CloudQualityScale). Together, because the texel is
// 2 x extent / resolution and the whole derivation below turns on that texel: scale them independently
// and the map stops resolving what the march can find, which is the one thing about this file that is not
// free to choose. The producer is therefore handed its extent-derived numbers on the push constant rather
// than compiling them from here — see Graphic::CloudShadowPush.
//
// THE DERIVATION RUNS RESOLUTION -> TEXEL -> EXTENT, not the other way round, and the middle term is not
// free to choose. A texel finer than the finest cloud chord the march can FIND stores detail the producer
// cannot make; a texel coarser than it throws away detail the sky visibly has. That chord is
// CloudResolvableChordKm(CLOUD_DISTANCE_TO_MAX_STEPS_KM / MaxSteps) = 0.125 km at the component's default
// ceiling of 256 steps. So:
//
//     512 texels x 0.125 km = 64 km across  ->  an extent (RADIUS) of 32 km, rounded to 30.
//
// and the texel lands at 0.1172 km, just inside the chord. Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudShadow asserts that
// relation rather than the two numbers, because it is the relation that was reasoned about.
//
// 512 IS UNREAL'S OWN DEFAULT (`512 * CloudShadowMapResolutionScale`, VolumetricCloudRendering.cpp:429)
// and it is also where the cost sits: the pass marches every texel through the shell, so the cost is
// quadratic in this number and 1024 would be four times the price for detail the march cannot produce.
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_RESOLUTION 512.0f

// The RADIUS of the square the map covers, in kilometres — half its side. Follows from the line above.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS: world geometry further than this from the camera receives no cloud shadow at all. That
// is a real limit and it is stated rather than hidden; CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BORDER_FADE_KM below is what keeps
// it from being a visible square edge on a large terrain. Unreal's answer to the same limit is a 150 km
// default extent, which at 512 texels is 586 m per texel — five times coarser than a cloud chord, and a
// deliberately different trade for a renderer whose clouds are visible at 150 km. Ours are not: the
// component's MaxViewDistance is 60 km.
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_EXTENT_KM 30.0f

// The world grid the map's centre is snapped to. Unreal's r.VolumetricCloud.ShadowMap.SnapLength, same
// twenty kilometres.
//
// WHY IT IS HERE AT ALL, and it is the whole reason this file has a snap function. The map is rebuilt
// every frame from a WORLD-SPACE function. If its projection drifts with the camera, the same piece of
// world lands in a different texel every frame, the bilinear fetch under it moves, and the shadow BOILS
// in place — a defect that is invisible in a still frame by construction and unmissable the moment the
// camera translates. Snapping the centre to a coarse grid means the projection is bit-for-bit identical
// over long stretches of camera motion, so the shadow is nailed to the world.
//
// The centre lands within half a snap of the camera, so the guaranteed coverage around the camera is
// EXTENT - SNAP/2 = 20 km rather than the full 30.
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_SNAP_KM 20.0f

// Samples taken across the shell by one texel's ray, and the multiplier applied as the sun reaches the
// horizon — Unreal's RaySampleMaxCount / RaySampleHorizonMultiplier pair
// (VolumetricCloudRendering.cpp:1890-1895), whose reason is stated there and is worth repeating: at a low
// sun each ray has far further to travel through the layer, and fewer texels have any layer over them at
// all, so raising the count keeps the cost and the quality both roughly where they were.
//
// THIRTY-TWO because that is what puts the sample spacing at the resolvable chord as well: the shipped
// congestus envelope is 3.6 km thick, and 3.6 / 32 = 0.1125 km. A count that resolves the map's texel
// horizontally and steps past a cloud vertically would be a map whose two axes disagree about what a
// cloud is.
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BASE_SAMPLES 32.0f
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_HORIZON_MULTIPLIER 2.0f

// The WORLD width of the band at the map's border over which the shadow is faded out to fully lit,
// kilometres. 1.2 km is ten texels at the reference tier — a gradient rather than an edge at every
// elevation a camera can look along the ground at.
//
// KILOMETRES RATHER THAN UV, which is what the quality tier made necessary and what the number always
// meant. 1.2 km over the reference tier's 60 km side is 0.02 in UV, which is what this used to be
// written as, so the shipped picture does not move by a digit. But the quantities that decide whether a
// fade READS as a fade are world quantities — how wide the gradient is on the ground, and how many texels
// it spans so it does not band — and a fixed UV keeps neither when the map covers half as much world: it
// would halve the band to five texels and put a visible ring exactly where the gradient was meant to hide
// one. Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudShadow asserts the ten-texel span on EVERY tier.
//
// Unreal does NOT do this and does not need to: at a 150 km extent the border is past its own fog. Ours
// is at 30 km, which is inside the far plane of every scene in this repository, so without the fade the
// map's boundary is a straight line of light across the ground — a defect that looks like a bug in the
// terrain rather than like a limit of the shadow.
#define CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BORDER_FADE_KM 1.2f

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The sizes that follow from the four above. Functions rather than more #defines, so the test drives the
// arithmetic the renderer performs instead of restating it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The world size of one texel, in kilometres. The quantity that has to stay inside the march's
// resolvable chord.
float CloudShadowTexelKm( float extentKm, float resolution )
{
    return 2.0f * extentKm / max( resolution, 1.0f );
}

// The border fade as the shader wants it — a fraction of the map's UV range — from the world width above
// and the map's actual side. ONE expression for a quantity two sides need, so a tier that changes the
// extent cannot leave the two disagreeing about how wide the gradient is.
float CloudShadowBorderFadeUv( float extentKm )
{
    return CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BORDER_FADE_KM / max( 2.0f * extentKm, 1e-3f );
}

// How far up the light ray the map's near plane is placed above the anchor point. Unreal's
// `LightDistance = SphereRadius * 2` (VolumetricCloudRendering.cpp:1819). It has to clear the top of the
// layer for every texel including the corners, and twice the extent does so by a wide margin at every
// sun elevation: 60 km against a shell that tops out below 15 km.
float CloudShadowLightDistanceKm( float extentKm )
{
    return 2.0f * extentKm;
}

// The depth the map's clip-space z spans, in kilometres — Unreal's `FarPlane = LightDistance * 2`. It is
// the scale that turns a sampled z back into a distance along the light ray, so it is the ONE number the
// consumer needs besides the matrix.
//
// EXPRESSED THROUGH THE NEAR PLANE RATHER THAN AS `4.0f * extentKm`, which is what it said until
// `-Wunused-function` pointed out that `CloudShadowLightDistanceKm` above had no callers at all. The
// comment already claimed the relation — far = 2 x light distance — while the code stated the product
// independently, so the two could drift apart with nothing to notice: change the near plane's factor
// and the far plane silently stops being twice it. Same number, and now the relation is the code.
float CloudShadowFarDepthKm( float extentKm )
{
    return 2.0f * CloudShadowLightDistanceKm( extentKm );
}

// One component of the map centre, snapped to the world grid. Unreal's
// `floor((v + 0.5 * snap) / snap) * snap` (VolumetricCloudRendering.cpp:1843-1845) — the half-snap offset
// is there so the lattice does not have a cell boundary sitting exactly on the world origin, which is
// where scenes put things.
float CloudShadowSnapToGrid( float value, float snapKm )
{
    float snap = max( snapKm, 1e-4f );
    return floor( ( value + 0.5f * snap ) / snap ) * snap;
}

// One clip-space component of a fixed world point, snapped to the map's own pixel lattice.
//
// WHY BOTH SNAPS AND NOT JUST THE GRID ONE. The grid snap freezes the map's CENTRE; it does not freeze
// its ORIENTATION, and the light direction rotates the whole basis whenever the sun moves. This is the
// residual: it moves the finished projection by up to half a texel so that the world origin lands on the
// lattice exactly, which pins every other world point to the lattice with it. Unreal's
// SnapComponentToPixelGrid (VolumetricCloudRendering.cpp:1862-1867).
//
// ONE TEXEL AND NOT TWO. Unreal halves its own half-resolution again with the comment "perhaps RT isn't
// traced at 1:1 ratio?", which is exactly what its own temporal path does — it traces the shadow map at
// half resolution and reconstructs (CloudShadowmapTracingSizeInvSize, :1790-1803). We do not take that
// path (§2.1: Epic's own temporal filter is "experimental and needs more work"), so our trace IS 1:1 and
// the lattice spacing is one texel: clip spans [-1,1] over `resolution` texels, so a texel is
// 2 / resolution and the snap quantum is its reciprocal.
float CloudShadowSnapToPixelGrid( float clipComponent, float resolution )
{
    float halfResolution = max( resolution, 1.0f ) * 0.5f;
    return floor( clipComponent * halfResolution + 0.5f ) / halfResolution;
}

// How many samples this frame's ray takes. `sunUpDot` is the cosine between the planet's up at the map
// centre and the direction TOWARD the sun — i.e. the sine of the sun's elevation.
float CloudShadowSampleCount( float sunUpDot )
{
    float horizon = clamp( 0.2f / max( abs( sunUpDot ), 1e-4f ), 0.0f, 1.0f );
    float extra   = max( CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_HORIZON_MULTIPLIER - 1.0f, 0.0f );
    return CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BASE_SAMPLES + extra * CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BASE_SAMPLES * horizon;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The texel
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct CloudShadowTexel
{
    float FrontDepthKm;        // along the light ray, from the map's near plane
    float MeanExtinctionPerKm; // averaged over the samples that found material, and over those only
    float MaxOpticalDepth;     // of the whole ray across the shell
};

vec3 CloudShadowEncode( CloudShadowTexel texel )
{
    vec3 encoded;
    encoded.x = max( texel.FrontDepthKm, 0.0f );
    encoded.y = max( texel.MeanExtinctionPerKm, 0.0f );
    encoded.z = max( texel.MaxOpticalDepth, 0.0f );
    return encoded;
}

CloudShadowTexel CloudShadowDecode( vec3 encoded )
{
    CloudShadowTexel texel;
    texel.FrontDepthKm        = encoded.x;
    texel.MeanExtinctionPerKm = encoded.y;
    texel.MaxOpticalDepth     = encoded.z;
    return texel;
}

// A texel that says "nothing here". Written by every ray that misses the shell, so that a fetch outside
// the cloud is a real answer rather than uninitialised device memory: the front depth is the far plane,
// which no receiver can be past, so the reconstruction below returns zero optical depth by its own
// arithmetic rather than by a special case.
CloudShadowTexel CloudShadowClearTexel( float farDepthKm )
{
    CloudShadowTexel texel;
    texel.FrontDepthKm        = farDepthKm;
    texel.MeanExtinctionPerKm = 0.0f;
    texel.MaxOpticalDepth     = 0.0f;
    return texel;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RECONSTRUCTION — the half of this file that the deferred lighting pass compiles
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Where a point sits along the light ray, in kilometres, given its clip-space z in the map.
//
// The map's projection is a PLAIN orthographic one with z running 0 at the near plane to 1 at the far
// plane (glm::orthoRH_ZO in Graphic::CloudBuildShadowMapView), so the distance is a straight product. It
// is NOT the engine's reversed-Z convention, and that is deliberate: reversed-Z buys float precision
// against a depth BUFFER, and this map has no depth buffer — its z is a number this file multiplies. The
// reference is reversed (`FReversedZOrthoMatrix`, hence its `1 - z`), and copying that here would have
// been one inversion carried through two shaders for no precision at all.
float CloudShadowDepthKm( float clipZ, float farDepthKm )
{
    return clamp( clipZ, 0.0f, 1.0f ) * farDepthKm;
}

// Clip xy to the map's UV. Written once because the producer stores through it and the consumer fetches
// through it, and a disagreement between the two is a shadow offset by a constant that reads as a bias
// problem.
vec2 CloudShadowClipToUv( vec2 clipXy )
{
    return clipXy * 0.5f + vec2( 0.5f, 0.5f );
}

// 1 well inside the map, falling to 0 at and outside its border. See CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BORDER_FADE_KM and
// CloudShadowBorderFadeUv above, which is where the @p fadeUv this takes comes from.
float CloudShadowBorderFade( vec2 uv, float fadeUv )
{
    float edge = min( min( uv.x, 1.0f - uv.x ), min( uv.y, 1.0f - uv.y ) );
    return clamp( edge / max( fadeUv, 1e-5f ), 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

// THE RECONSTRUCTION ITSELF. Optical depth from the map at a point `sampleDepthKm` along the ray.
//
// Three cases fall out of the two terms without a branch, which is why the encoding is worth the trouble:
//   * a receiver ABOVE the front of the cloud   -> the max() is 0, no shade;
//   * a receiver INSIDE the layer               -> the mean extinction over the depth it has actually
//                                                  travelled through, which is the case an occluder-depth
//                                                  shadow map cannot express at all;
//   * a receiver BELOW the layer (the ground)   -> the min() clamps to the whole ray's optical depth, so
//                                                  the mean extinction is not extrapolated through the
//                                                  clear air under the cloud.
float CloudShadowOpticalDepth( CloudShadowTexel texel, float sampleDepthKm )
{
    float insideKm = max( 0.0f, sampleDepthKm - texel.FrontDepthKm );
    return min( texel.MaxOpticalDepth, texel.MeanExtinctionPerKm * insideKm );
}

// The transmittance a receiver sees, with the artist's strength applied.
//
// STRENGTH IS APPLIED HERE AND NOT BAKED INTO THE MAP, which is where this parts company with the
// reference (Unreal scales both stored channels by CloudShadowStrength at write time,
// VolumetricCloud.usf:2211-2212). Applied at read time the map holds the medium's own physical numbers,
// the knob is one multiply in one place, and moving it does not invalidate a map that is otherwise a pure
// function of the weather. The rendered result is identical — the scale commutes with everything between
// the two points, including the min() above, because both of its arguments are scaled by the same factor.
float CloudShadowTransmittance( vec3 encoded, float sampleDepthKm, float strength )
{
    CloudShadowTexel texel = CloudShadowDecode( encoded );
    float            tau   = CloudShadowOpticalDepth( texel, sampleDepthKm ) * max( strength, 0.0f );
    return clamp( exp( -tau ), 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PRODUCER — compiled only where a density is available
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Guarded because this header has two very different readers. Programs/Clouds/CloudShadowMap.shader
// defines the callback and gets the march; Programs/Deferred/DeferredLighting.shader does not define it
// and gets only the reconstruction above, with no cloud field, no noise volume and no profile table
// anywhere near the deferred pass. Same arrangement as Common/SkyScattering.glslh, whose integrator stays
// inert in a pass that defines neither LUT callback.
//
// CLOUD_SHADOW_SAMPLE_EXTINCTION(posKm) must return the medium's extinction, per kilometre, at a
// PLANET-CENTRE-RELATIVE position in kilometres — zero outside the shell.

#ifdef CLOUD_SHADOW_SAMPLE_EXTINCTION

// Marches one texel's ray across the shell and produces its triple.
//
// UNIFORM SPACING, unlike the light march inside the cloud pass, which puts its samples on a squared
// distribution. The reason the two differ is what they are for: the light march integrates outward from a
// point where nearly all of the material is, so it needs its resolution at the near end; this ray is a
// slice through the whole layer with no privileged end, and a squared distribution here would resolve the
// top of the deck and step over its base. It is Unreal's arrangement too (`Linear shadow samples
// (reference)`, VolumetricCloud.usf:2181-2182).
//
// @param originKm  the ray's start, planet-centre-relative kilometres — the map's near plane.
// @param lightDir  the direction the light TRAVELS, normalized.
// @param tEnterKm  entry into the shell along @p lightDir, from CloudLayerIntersect.
// @param tExitKm   exit from it. A segment with tExitKm <= tEnterKm is a miss and yields the clear texel.
CloudShadowTexel CloudShadowTraceRay( vec3 originKm, vec3 lightDir, float tEnterKm, float tExitKm,
                                      int sampleCount, float farDepthKm )
{
    CloudShadowTexel texel = CloudShadowClearTexel( farDepthKm );

    if ( tExitKm <= tEnterKm || sampleCount < 1 )
        return texel;

    float lengthKm = tExitKm - tEnterKm;
    float stepKm   = lengthKm / float( sampleCount );

    float extinctionSum = 0.0f;
    float hitCount      = 0.0f;
    float opticalDepth  = 0.0f;
    // NEGATIVE IS THE "not found yet" SENTINEL, and it cannot collide with an answer because a distance
    // along the ray from its own entry point is never negative. A sentinel of `farDepthKm` would collide
    // with a genuine hit at the far plane.
    float frontKm = -1.0f;

    for ( int i = 0; i < sampleCount; ++i )
    {
        float offsetKm = lengthKm * ( ( float( i ) + 0.5f ) / float( sampleCount ) );
        vec3  posKm    = originKm + lightDir * ( tEnterKm + offsetKm );

        float sigma = CLOUD_SHADOW_SAMPLE_EXTINCTION( posKm );

        if ( sigma > 0.0f )
        {
            if ( frontKm < 0.0f )
                frontKm = offsetKm;

            extinctionSum = extinctionSum + sigma;
            hitCount      = hitCount + 1.0f;
        }

        opticalDepth = opticalDepth + sigma * stepKm;
    }

    // THE MEAN IS OVER THE SAMPLES THAT FOUND MATERIAL, not over all of them, and that is the pairing
    // that makes the reconstruction correct rather than merely plausible: it is multiplied by the depth
    // travelled PAST THE FRONT of the cloud, so it has to be the extinction of the cloud and not the
    // extinction of the whole chord including the clear air above it. Averaging over every sample would
    // make a thin cloud in a tall shell arbitrarily transparent as the shell grew.
    texel.FrontDepthKm        = frontKm < 0.0f ? tExitKm : ( tEnterKm + frontKm );
    texel.MeanExtinctionPerKm = extinctionSum / max( 1.0f, hitCount );
    texel.MaxOpticalDepth     = opticalDepth;
    return texel;
}

#endif // CLOUD_SHADOW_SAMPLE_EXTINCTION

#endif // CLOUD_SHADOW_MAP_GLSLH
